  "summary": "Four documents directly apply boosted decision-tree ensembles to wildfire mapping, with one additional wildfire-mapping study using a different method included to round out the five. [V0184] (Fischer et al., 2022) applies boosted decision-tree ensembles to mapping active wildfire extent on the Atlas-ICU dataset, achieving a test F1 of 0.93. [V0183] (El-Sayed et al., 2021) applies the same method to wildfire extent mapping on the MetroFlow dataset, reporting AUROC of 0.86. [V0181] (Chen et al., 2019) revisits wildfire extent mapping with boosted decision-tree ensembles on the FireScope dataset, achieving accuracy of 0.72. [V0182] (Duarte et al., 2020) applies boosted decision-tree ensembles to wildfire extent mapping on the ChurnBench dataset, reporting MAE of 0.79. [V0185] (Duarte et al., 2020) addresses the same wildfire mapping topic but uses multilevel probabilistic models with partial pooling on FireScope, reporting MAE of 0.83; it is included as the fifth-ranked document since no additional gradient-boosted-tree wildfire studies remain in the candidate set. Documents such as [V0087], [V0229], [V0016], and [V0160] use boosted decision-tree ensembles but apply them to unrelated tasks, while documents like [V0044], [V0027], [V0209], and others only mention wildfire mapping as prior work rather than as their primary application.",
